rd word, then its complexity satisfies C_u(n) < 6n for all n ∈ N.\nINTEGERS: 17 (2017) 26\n\n• It might be helpful to illustrate what the situation is for sufficiently long left special factors of the Thue–Morse word and what seems to be the situation for the word u_p. See Figure 1. In both cases, there are two infinite left special branches – the infinite word itself and its ER-image, i.e., the word that arises when exchanging ones with zeroes. The weak bispecial factors form finite branches and the common prefixes of the weak bispecials and the infinite left special branch correspond to strong bispecials. The first difference of complexity ΔC(n) equals to the number of left special factors of length n. In the case of u_p in contrast to u_TM, the detached parts of finite branches may overlap, thus the first difference of complexity is larger. The question is whether it is possible to construct an example where there are even more overlapping detached parts of finite branches.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 1 diagram comparing infinite left special factors of u_p and u_TM. Top row labeled u_p shows a horizontal branch with blue upper curves labeled E(s_{4k+1}) and E(s_{4k+5}), red lower curves labeled R(s_{4k+3}) and R(s_{4k+7}), and three annotations ΔC_{u_p} = 3. Bottom row labeled u_TM shows a horizontal branch with purple upper curves and green lower curves, with four annotations ΔC_{u_TM} = 2. Caption states that only one of two infinite left special branches is illustrated in each case, so the total first difference of complexity has to be doubled.\n</visual_element>\n\nFigure 1: Infinite left special factors of u_p and u_TM. We illustrate in each case only one of two infinite left special branches. Hence, the total first difference of complexity has to be doubled.\n\n• Let us state a new conjecture based on our computer experiments:\n\nConjecture 2 (Conjecture 6n). Let u be a binary generalized pseudostandard word, then its complexity satisfies C_u(n) 